// CRES Academy question bank, units 5 to 8. All questions are original.
// Fields: id, t (lesson id), q (stem), a (four options), c (index of correct option), e (explanation).
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u5l1
{id:"u5l1-1",t:"u5l1",q:"A trainee asks why a hand technique at 60 kVp produces no characteristic radiation from a tungsten target. What is the correct answer?",a:["Characteristic radiation needs a rotating anode and this exposure used the stationary mode","Characteristic radiation only appears above the tungsten K-shell energy of about 69.5 keV","Added filtration removes all characteristic photons below 70 kVp","Characteristic radiation is produced only at the small focal spot"],c:1,e:"A characteristic photon carries the difference between two binding energies, and only tungsten K-shell transitions near 69.5 keV are diagnostically useful. Below that tube potential the electrons cannot remove a K-shell electron, so the beam is all bremsstrahlung."},
{id:"u5l1-2",t:"u5l1",q:"Output on a room has drifted low at every mA station while measured kVp is correct. Which part of the generator sets the number of electrons crossing the tube?",a:["The autotransformer tap selection","The rectifier bank","The filament circuit, which controls filament heating","The step-up transformer turns ratio"],c:2,e:"Tube current is set by how hot the filament runs, and the filament circuit controls that heating. A small change in filament current makes a large change in mA, which is why mA calibration lives there rather than in the high-voltage section."},
{id:"u5l1-3",t:"u5l1",q:"A radiographer reports that long field images are consistently lighter at one end even though the technique is unchanged. What explains the gradient?",a:["Grid cutoff from an off-level grid","Scatter fog building up along one edge","A failing detector row at that edge","The heel effect, which lowers intensity toward the anode end of the field"],c:3,e:"Photons leaving toward the anode end travel out through more target material, so intensity falls on that side. It is normal and it is used on purpose by placing the thickest part of the anatomy under the cathode side."},
{id:"u5l1-4",t:"u5l1",q:"Asked in a budget meeting why imaging tubes need so much cooling hardware, what is the correct physical reason?",a:["Only about 1 percent of the electrons' energy becomes x-rays and about 99 percent becomes heat","High voltage insulation must be kept below room temperature","The vacuum envelope generates heat on its own during rotation","Rectifiers dissipate most of the energy before it reaches the tube"],c:0,e:"X-ray production is a very inefficient process: roughly 1 percent of the kinetic energy delivered to the target leaves as x-rays and the rest becomes heat in a spot a millimeter or two across. Every rating chart and cooling curve exists because of that ratio."},
{id:"u5l1-5",t:"u5l1",q:"A tube specification lists a focal track of about 10 mm by 1 mm but a nominal focal spot of about 1 mm square. Which design principle accounts for the difference?",a:["The space charge effect","The line-focus principle, because the angled target projects a shorter spot","Positive beam limitation","Reciprocity between mA and time"],c:1,e:"Angling the target lets a long thermal track project as a small square when viewed from the receptor, so the tube takes heat like a big target and images like a small source. A steeper angle gives a smaller effective spot and a stronger heel effect."},

// u5l2
{id:"u5l2-1",t:"u5l2",q:"A single-phase room is used for a lumbar series at 80 kVp and 100 mAs. How much thermal loading does that one exposure put on the anode?",a:["800 HU","11,200 HU","8,000 HU","80,000 HU"],c:2,e:"On single-phase equipment heat units are kVp x mA x s, which is kVp x mAs: 80 x 100 = 8,000 HU. At about 1.4 J per heat unit that is roughly 11 kJ dumped into a focal track a few millimeters wide."},
{id:"u5l2-2",t:"u5l2",q:"The same exposure of 100 kVp and 50 mAs is made on a three-phase generator. What is the thermal loading?",a:["5,000 HU","3,570 HU","14,000 HU","7,000 HU"],c:3,e:"Three-phase and high-frequency equipment carries a 1.4 modification factor because low ripple holds the tube near peak voltage for more of the cycle: 1.4 x 100 x 50 = 7,000 HU. The same settings on single phase would be 5,000 HU."},
{id:"u5l2-3",t:"u5l2",q:"Technologists report that the rotor on a busy fluoroscopic room now whines and takes several seconds to reach ready. What does that MOST likely indicate?",a:["Bearing wear in the rotor assembly, which means the tube is near the end of its life","A failed stator capacitor that needs replacing","An interlock that is opening late","Low oil level in the housing"],c:0,e:"Heat conducted down the stem ruins the rotor bearings, and long fluoroscopic runs are the hardest service on them. A noisy rotor with a slow run-up, especially with output drift, is end-of-life evidence rather than a calibration issue."},
{id:"u5l2-4",t:"u5l2",q:"An older room with a glass-envelope tube begins throwing intermittent tube-arc and overcurrent faults at high technique. What is the likely cause?",a:["A shorted high-voltage cable termination only","Vaporized tungsten plated inside the envelope conducting across the vacuum","Dust in the collimator assembly","An exposure timer running long"],c:1,e:"Tungsten boiling off the filament and target plates the inside of a glass envelope, and that conductive film lets tube current jump the vacuum. The generator sees it as an arc or an overcurrent, and metal-envelope tubes exist largely to delay it."},
{id:"u5l2-5",t:"u5l2",q:"A portable unit that sat unused all weekend fails immediately when a technologist takes a high-technique abdomen with it. Which practice was most likely skipped?",a:["Discharging the capacitor bank before use","Reading the housing cooling curve","The manufacturer's warm-up series before heavy loading","Verifying the collimator lamp"],c:2,e:"A cold anode loaded hard can crack or begin to wobble and fails at once. The warm-up series in the manual raises anode temperature gradually, and it is the single cheapest way to protect an expensive tube."},

// u5l3
{id:"u5l3-1",t:"u5l3",q:"A service manual states that the tube potential on a generator never falls below about 86 percent of peak. Which waveform is described?",a:["Single-phase full wave","High frequency","Three-phase twelve pulse","Three-phase six pulse, with about 14 percent ripple"],c:3,e:"Six-pulse three-phase power has about 14 percent ripple, so the voltage stays near 86 percent of peak or above. Twelve pulse is about 4 percent ripple and stays above about 96 percent, and high frequency is about 1 percent."},
{id:"u5l3-2",t:"u5l3",q:"A technique chart copied from an old single-phase portable is loaded into a new high-frequency room, and every image comes out dark. Why?",a:["Lower ripple produces more x-rays and more penetrating ones for the same settings","The new room needs a higher grid ratio","High-frequency generators have a longer minimum exposure time","Digital detectors are slower than the film the chart was written for"],c:0,e:"Less voltage ripple keeps the tube near peak for more of the exposure, so quantity and quality both rise at the same kVp and mAs. Charts do not transfer between waveforms, and moving to low ripple can require as much as 10 kVp less for the same receptor exposure."},
{id:"u5l3-3",t:"u5l3",q:"An abdomen is being taken at 80 kVp and 40 mAs, and the radiologist asks for the same receptor exposure at lower patient dose. Which setting follows the 15 percent rule?",a:["92 kVp at 80 mAs","92 kVp at 20 mAs","68 kVp at 80 mAs","80 kVp at 20 mAs"],c:1,e:"Raising kVp 15 percent has about the effect of doubling the mAs, so raising kVp and halving mAs holds receptor exposure: 80 x 1.15 = 92 kVp, and 40 mAs halved is 20 mAs. The cost is lower contrast, because higher kVp is more penetrating."},
{id:"u5l3-4",t:"u5l3",q:"Measuring exposure timer accuracy, you find the 10 ms station reads 15 percent high and the 100 ms station reads 3 percent high. What is the verdict?",a:["Both fail and the generator must be recalibrated","The 10 ms station fails and the 100 ms station passes","Both pass: 20 percent is allowed at or below 10 ms and 5 percent above it","Timer tolerance does not apply below 50 ms"],c:2,e:"Exposure timer accuracy is held within 5 percent of the set time above 10 ms and within 20 percent at or below 10 ms, because very short times are harder to control. Record both measured values with the tolerance so the pass is documented."},
{id:"u5l3-5",t:"u5l3",q:"Two settings are compared during a linearity check: 200 mA at 0.1 s and 400 mA at 0.05 s. What should the dosimeter show?",a:["Twice the output at 400 mA, because current is doubled","Output that differs by the ratio of the two kVp settings","Half the output at 400 mA, because the time is halved","The same output, because both are 20 mAs and reciprocity should hold"],c:3,e:"Both combinations are 20 mAs, and any pair with the same product should give the same receptor exposure. Proving it is the linearity test, and adjacent mA stations must agree within 10 percent of output per mAs."},

// u5l4
{id:"u5l4-1",t:"u5l4",q:"During a quality control check you place lead over the selected exposure control chamber and fire. Where must the exposure stop?",a:["At 6 s or 600 mAs, whichever comes first","At 2 s or 200 mAs","At the minimum response time","When the anode reaches its rated heat"],c:0,e:"Starving the chamber forces the system onto its backup, which must end the exposure at 6 s or 600 mAs, whichever comes first. That test proves the patient and the tube are protected when the automatic control fails to terminate."},
{id:"u5l4-2",t:"u5l4",q:"Chest images from one room come out too light, and the technologists say they are using the automatic mode. What should you check FIRST?",a:["The high-voltage cable terminations","Whether lung rather than mediastinum is sitting over the selected chamber","The grid ratio in the wall stand","Detector gain calibration"],c:1,e:"The chamber ends the exposure on whatever lies over it, so air-filled lung fills it quickly and the exposure stops early. Reproduce it with a phantom before touching calibration, because off-cell anatomy is a positioning finding rather than an electronic fault."},
{id:"u5l4-3",t:"u5l4",q:"A technologist asks what one notch on the density selector actually does to an automatically timed exposure. What is the correct answer?",a:["It changes kVp by one station","It switches which chamber is selected","It moves the resulting image density by a small fixed step, typically about 0.1","It overrides the backup timer"],c:2,e:"The density control shifts the target the chamber is working to, and each step moves the resulting density by roughly 0.1. It is there for unusual patients and for slow drift in the control, not as a substitute for calibration."},
{id:"u5l4-4",t:"u5l4",q:"A radiologist wants more contrast on lumbar spine images from a room whose output measures correctly. Which changes serve that request?",a:["Raise kVp and remove the grid","Select the large focal spot","Lengthen the distance and raise mAs","Lower kVp and make sure the grid is in use with tight collimation"],c:3,e:"Contrast is controlled first by kVp and then by how much scatter reaches the receptor. Lower kVp widens the absorption difference, and a grid plus tight collimation removes the scatter that would otherwise fog the image."},
{id:"u5l4-5",t:"u5l4",q:"A dose review finds that abdomen exposures in one digital room are consistently high, yet the images look normal on the monitor. Which number reveals that?",a:["The deviation index, read against the target for that exam","The half-value layer","The Bucky factor","The grid conversion factor"],c:0,e:"Digital processing hides overexposure, so the detector's own report is the honest measure: the exposure index says what arrived and the deviation index says how far that is from the target. A persistently positive deviation index is overexposure even when every image looks fine."},

// u5l5
{id:"u5l5-1",t:"u5l5",q:"Checking collimator accuracy with the tube set 100 cm from the receptor, how far may the light field edge differ from the radiation field edge?",a:["5 cm","2 cm, because the tolerance is 2 percent of the distance","1 mm","10 cm"],c:1,e:"The light field must agree with the radiation field within 2 percent of the source-to-image distance, which is 2 cm at 100 cm. Automatic collimation and the distance indicator are held to the same 2 percent."},
{id:"u5l5-2",t:"u5l5",q:"A state inspector asks you to demonstrate that a room operating at 90 kVp has adequate total filtration. How is that proven?",a:["By removing the collimator and counting the aluminum sheets installed","By reading the inherent filtration from the tube nameplate","By measuring the half-value layer and comparing it with the minimum for that kVp and waveform","By measuring tube current at the console"],c:2,e:"Inherent and added filtration cannot be measured separately once the tube is assembled, so beam quality stands in for both: add calibrated aluminum until intensity halves and compare with the published minimum. Above 70 kVp total filtration must be at least 2.5 mm of aluminum equivalent."},
{id:"u5l5-3",t:"u5l5",q:"Images from a portable grid cassette show density fading along both long edges with normal density in the middle. What happened?",a:["The grid is off level","Scatter fog from an open collimator","The exposure was too short for the chamber","The focused grid was installed upside down"],c:3,e:"A focused grid has angled strips that must face the tube on its labeled side; reversed, the angled strips cut the diverging beam at both edges. Off level drops density across the whole image and off center drops it at the sides, so the pattern names the mistake."},
{id:"u5l5-4",t:"u5l5",q:"A department adds a 12:1 grid to a room that previously imaged without one and now complains that every image is too light. What should you explain?",a:["A grid absorbs primary radiation as well as scatter, so the mAs must rise by the Bucky factor","The grid has changed the half-value layer of the beam","Grids reduce kVp at the tube","A grid lowers detector sensitivity"],c:0,e:"The grid conversion factor, also called the Bucky factor, is the mAs multiplier a grid costs, and it rises with ratio and with kVp. The exposure must be raised to compensate, which is the dose price paid for the contrast improvement of about 1.5 to 2.5."},
{id:"u5l5-5",t:"u5l5",q:"A neuroradiology room images lateral cervical spines with a long gap between the patient and the receptor and no grid at all. Why does that work?",a:["The gap filters low-energy photons out of the beam","Scattered photons diverge past the receptor, so contrast is preserved without a grid","A long gap reduces the heel effect","The chamber reacts faster across an air gap"],c:1,e:"The air gap technique lets scatter, which leaves the patient at an angle, miss the receptor instead of being absorbed by a grid, so no extra mAs is needed. The cost is magnification and more focal spot blur, both of which grow with the gap."},

// u5l6
{id:"u5l6-1",t:"u5l6",q:"Every table image from one room now shows fine parallel lines across the anatomy, while wall stand images are clean. What is the FIRST thing to check?",a:["The collimator lamp alignment","The detector defective pixel map","Whether the table Bucky grid is still oscillating during the exposure","Line voltage at the main disconnect"],c:2,e:"A moving grid blurs its own strip shadows away, so visible lines mean the Bucky drive has stalled or the grid is stationary. Watch and listen for the oscillation during an exposure before suspecting anything in the image chain."},
{id:"u5l6-2",t:"u5l6",q:"Inspecting a ceiling-mounted tube support, you find several broken strands in the counterweight cable. What is the correct action?",a:["Note it for the next scheduled visit","Lubricate the cable and continue the inspection","Reduce the travel limits to lower the load","Take the room out of service and replace the cable before it is used again"],c:3,e:"A counterweight cable carries the tube over the patient, so a frayed cable is a crush hazard rather than a maintenance note. Broken strands condemn a cable, and the room stays locked out until it is replaced and the travel proven."},
{id:"u5l6-3",t:"u5l6",q:"A room's distance indicator reads 100 cm while a tape measure from focal spot to receptor shows 104 cm. Does the indicator pass?",a:["No: the indicator must be within 2 percent, which is 2 cm at this distance","Yes, because indicators are advisory","Yes, if the technique chart is rewritten to match","No, but only if automatic collimation is fitted"],c:0,e:"The distance indicator is held within 2 percent of the source-to-image distance, so 2 cm at 100 cm; 4 cm of error fails. Every technique chart assumes the indicated distance, so an error there changes receptor exposure on every exam in the room."},
{id:"u5l6-4",t:"u5l6",q:"Technologists complain that centering is inconsistent in one room, though image quality is otherwise fine. Which mechanical fault fits that description best?",a:["A failed collision sensor","Detents that no longer land cleanly at the standard positions","Worn rotor bearings","A stalled Bucky drive"],c:1,e:"Detents are the click-stops that make a standard position repeatable, and when they drift or weaken each setup lands slightly differently. The complaint arrives as inconsistency rather than as a mechanical fault, so prove the detents and the indicators before discussing technique."},
{id:"u5l6-5",t:"u5l6",q:"A preventive maintenance procedure lists the collision sensors on a powered table and stand. How should those items be completed?",a:["Confirm the sensors are wired and move on","Check the error log for collision faults","Trip each sensor and confirm that powered motion actually stops","Measure the supply voltage to the drive"],c:2,e:"A safety device is only verified by making it act, so each sensor, limit switch, and emergency stop is tripped and the motion confirmed to stop. Record what was tested, because an untested interlock is the same as a missing one to a surveyor."},

// u6l1
{id:"u6l1-1",t:"u6l1",q:"One computed radiography cassette produces the same small white mark in the same corner of every image it makes. What is the cause?",a:["A defective pixel in the reader's photodetector","A failing laser in the reader","An incomplete erase cycle","Dust or a scratch on the storage phosphor plate itself"],c:3,e:"The plate is a physical receptor, so anything on or in it prints in the same place on every image made with that cassette. Inspect and clean the plate, and retire it if the surface is scratched or cracked."},
{id:"u6l1-2",t:"u6l1",q:"A technologist reports a faint second image of the previous patient's anatomy showing behind a new study on a plate system. Which step failed?",a:["The erase lamp did not fully clear the plate before reuse","The laser scan ran too fast","The reader's photodetector lost gain","The plate was exposed twice in the same cassette"],c:0,e:"After reading, a bright lamp floods the plate to release whatever energy is still trapped. An incomplete erase leaves a ghost of the earlier exposure, which is a reader fault rather than a technique error."},
{id:"u6l1-3",t:"u6l1",q:"Comparing two flat panel detectors, one is described as indirect and the other as direct capture. What is the difference?",a:["Indirect panels are wireless and direct panels are wired","Indirect panels convert x-rays to light in cesium iodide first; direct panels make charge in amorphous selenium with no light step","Indirect panels read out faster because they skip the transistor array","Direct panels require a grid and indirect panels do not"],c:1,e:"An indirect detector uses a scintillator over an amorphous silicon transistor array, so x-rays become light and light becomes charge. A direct detector uses amorphous selenium to make charge from x-rays with no intermediate light stage."},
{id:"u6l1-4",t:"u6l1",q:"A vendor claims its new detector gives the same image quality at lower patient dose. Which specification supports that claim?",a:["A thinner protective cover","A larger pixel pitch","A higher detective quantum efficiency","A higher grid ratio"],c:2,e:"Detective quantum efficiency describes how much of the radiation reaching the detector becomes usable signal, so a higher value means the same image for less exposure. It varies with photon energy, so the comparison only means something at a stated technique."},
{id:"u6l1-5",t:"u6l1",q:"A small clinic still processes film and asks what its daily sensitometric strip is actually monitoring. What is the answer?",a:["Tube output and timer accuracy","Screen phosphor efficiency only","Grid alignment and collimation","Film speed, contrast, and base plus fog against the established baseline"],c:3,e:"A step strip is exposed with a sensitometer and read with a densitometer, and the speed index, the density difference, and base plus fog are compared with baseline, typically within about 0.10 for the first two. It monitors the whole processing chain, not the x-ray machine."},

// u6l2
{id:"u6l2-1",t:"u6l2",q:"Images from a wireless panel show a single dark vertical line in the same pixel column no matter what is imaged. What should you suspect?",a:["A dead data line or a defective pixel map that is out of date","Patient motion during readout","Scatter from an open collimator","A grid frequency beating against the detector"],c:0,e:"The array is read through gate lines by row and data lines by column, so an electronics fault is straight and never moves with anatomy. Recalibrate and update the pixel map; if the line remains, the readout hardware is the fault."},
{id:"u6l2-2",t:"u6l2",q:"After a generator repair that changed the added filtration, panel images show a broad brightness gradient across the field. What is the FIRST corrective step?",a:["Replace the detector","Repeat the gain or flat-field calibration for the new beam","Increase the exposure index target","Change the look-up table"],c:1,e:"Gain calibration teaches the panel how each pixel responds to a known uniform beam, so changing the beam invalidates it and the error appears as shading. Offset, gain, and the pixel map are all void after a filtration, board, panel, or firmware change."},
{id:"u6l2-3",t:"u6l2",q:"You have just installed a replacement flat panel in a general room. Which calibration sequence does the manufacturer expect?",a:["Gain, then offset, then the pixel map","The pixel map alone, since the panel ships calibrated","Offset, then gain, then the defective pixel map","Offset only, with gain performed annually"],c:2,e:"Offset sets each pixel's zero with no radiation, gain sets its scale against a uniform exposure, and the defective pixel map then records what cannot be corrected. Each step depends on the one before it, so the order is not optional."},
{id:"u6l2-4",t:"u6l2",q:"A radiologist asks you to widen the gray scale on a reading station so more tissue types show at once. Which control does that?",a:["Window level, which sets displayed brightness","The exposure index target","The edge enhancement filter","Window width, which sets how many values are spread across the gray scale"],c:3,e:"Window width is the number of image values mapped across the gray scale, so a wider window shows more tissue types with less contrast between them. Window level moves where that window sits, which changes displayed brightness."},
{id:"u6l2-5",t:"u6l2",q:"A portable panel carried in from a cold vehicle produces noisy, uneven images for the first few exposures, then settles. What is happening?",a:["The panel is outside its working temperature and has not stabilized","The battery is failing under load","The radio link is dropping packets","The scintillator has delaminated"],c:0,e:"Panels have a working temperature range and need time to stabilize, because offset and gain both drift with temperature. Tell the department rather than recalibrating in the cold, and record it as a handling note."},

// u6l3
{id:"u6l3-1",t:"u6l3",q:"An intensifier tube has a 25 cm input phosphor, a 2.5 cm output phosphor, and a flux gain of 50. What is its brightness gain?",a:["500","5,000","1,250","2,500"],c:1,e:"Minification gain is the square of the diameter ratio: 25 / 2.5 = 10, and 10 squared is 100. Brightness gain is minification gain times flux gain, so 100 x 50 = 5,000, which sits at the low end of the usual 5,000 to 30,000."},
{id:"u6l3-2",t:"u6l3",q:"A fluoroscope measures a much higher tabletop dose rate than its baseline, and the operator has been working in the smallest field size. Why does that raise dose?",a:["The smaller field increases the flux gain","Collimating the beam increases its average energy","A smaller field uses less of the input phosphor, so minification gain falls and the system adds radiation to hold brightness","Magnification modes bypass the brightness control"],c:2,e:"Minification gain is the square of the input-to-output diameter ratio, so selecting a smaller input field cuts it sharply. Brightness control defends the picture, not the dose, so it compensates with more radiation, which is why magnification buys resolution with milligray."},
{id:"u6l3-3",t:"u6l3",q:"You are asked to verify that a fluoroscope without a high-level control meets the federal exposure rate requirement. What is the limit at the tabletop?",a:["Not above 2 R/min","Not above 5 R/min","Not above 20 R/min","Not above 10 R/min"],c:3,e:"In normal operation the rate measured at the tabletop is held to 10 R/min or below, and 20 R/min is allowed only where a high-level control is fitted, with no limit while images are being recorded. Measure it with a calibrated chamber."},
{id:"u6l3-4",t:"u6l3",q:"During a fluoroscopic quality control visit a technologist asks what the audible signal partway through a long case is for. What do you tell her?",a:["It marks 5 minutes of cumulative beam-on time so the operator is aware of the dose","It warns that the tube has reached its heat limit","It signals that the backup timer has ended the exposure","It indicates the brightness control has reached its limit"],c:0,e:"A cumulative timer must sound at 5 minutes of beam-on time, and the beam-on time is recorded for the exam. It is a dose awareness requirement, separate from the tube's thermal protection."},
{id:"u6l3-5",t:"u6l3",q:"Radiologists report that one fluoroscopic room gives a dim image with curved distortion near the edges and washed out contrast. What does that pattern point to?",a:["A flat panel detector with a stale gain calibration","An aging image intensifier losing brightness gain, with vignetting and veiling glare","A grid left out of the beam path","An exposure control chamber out of calibration"],c:1,e:"Vignetting, peripheral geometric distortion, and veiling glare belong to intensifier optics and get worse with age, while brightness gain falls and the dose control compensates. Flat-panel fluoroscopes do not produce those artifacts, which is one reason they replace intensifiers."},

// u6l4
{id:"u6l4-1",t:"u6l4",q:"A mobile C-arm loses its image only when the arm is swung to a steep oblique, then works normally again. How do you find the fault?",a:["Replace the monitor cart processor","Recalibrate the flat panel","Run a continuity check on the interconnect cable while the arm is moved through the position","Measure the battery under load"],c:2,e:"A fault that follows motion is a flexing conductor, and a static measurement will pass. Wiggle testing continuity through the suspect positions finds a broken strand that only opens at a particular bend."},
{id:"u6l4-2",t:"u6l4",q:"In the middle of a case, the staff report that a draped C-arm's orbital brake slips and the arm drifts toward the sterile field. What is the correct response?",a:["Advise the team to hold the arm by hand for the rest of the case","Note it on the next preventive maintenance visit","Tighten the brake through the drape","Remove the unit from service after the case and tag it, because a slipping brake is a safety and sterility fault"],c:3,e:"Positioning on a C-arm is held by brakes, so one that slips can contaminate a field or strike a patient. Document it, tag the unit out, and repair the brake before the next case rather than treating it as wear."},
{id:"u6l4-3",t:"u6l4",q:"Two dose numbers are displayed on an interventional system, and one of them drops when the operator collimates tighter. Which one is it?",a:["Dose area product, because it multiplies dose by the irradiated field area","Reference air kerma, because it is measured at a fixed point","Fluoroscopy time","Peak skin dose"],c:0,e:"Dose area product counts the volume of tissue irradiated, so narrowing the field lowers it even when the dose rate at a point does not change. Reference air kerma is the cumulative dose at a defined reference point and does not fall with collimation."},
{id:"u6l4-4",t:"u6l4",q:"Before an angiographic case the team confirms emergency drugs and suction are in the room. Which fact justifies that routine with modern contrast agents?",a:["Low-osmolality nonionic agents eliminate reactions","About 3 percent of patients still react to low-osmolality nonionic agents","Reactions only occur with barium studies","Reactions occur only in patients with shellfish allergy"],c:1,e:"Nonionic low-osmolality agents are far safer than the older ionic agents but not reaction-free, with roughly 3 percent of patients still reacting. The room therefore keeps emergency supplies ready, and the technician keeps the supporting equipment working and charged."},
{id:"u6l4-5",t:"u6l4",q:"A battery-powered mobile unit holds a full charge indication but dies partway through a shift. What measurement settles it?",a:["Open-circuit voltage at rest","Charger output voltage only","Pack voltage and current under load, with the run time logged","Insulation resistance of the pack"],c:2,e:"A tired pack shows normal voltage at rest and collapses when current is drawn, so capacity is only visible under load. Measure under load, log the achievable run time, and compare it with the specification before replacing the charger."},

// u6l5
{id:"u6l5-1",t:"u6l5",q:"Testing a mammography unit with a scale under the paddle, the powered mode peaks at 22 lb. What is the verdict?",a:["It passes, because any force above 20 lb is adequate","Compression force is not a technician test","It passes only if the manual mode reaches 40 lb","It fails: both powered and manual modes must reach between 25 and 40 lb"],c:3,e:"Powered and manual compression must both reach 25 to 40 pounds and hold for at least 15 seconds. Record the measured force with the tolerance, because a compression complaint closed without a number is not closed."},
{id:"u6l5-2",t:"u6l5",q:"On the same unit the automatic mode drives the paddle to 45 lb before releasing. Why is that a finding?",a:["The automatic mode must never exceed 40 lb","Any force above 30 lb damages the detector","Forty-five pounds is acceptable if the patient consents","The paddle only needs recalibration if it reads below 25 lb"],c:0,e:"Compression must never exceed 40 pounds automatically, which protects the patient from injury and the paddle from damage. The limit is part of the compression test, measured with a scale under the paddle."},
{id:"u6l5-3",t:"u6l5",q:"Asked why a mammography unit runs at only about 23 to 28 kVp when a chest room uses over 100, what is the reason?",a:["The small focal spot cannot carry higher voltage","Low photon energy maximizes the photoelectric effect, which is what gives soft tissue contrast","The detector saturates above 30 kVp","Compression limits the usable tube potential"],c:1,e:"Breast tissues differ very little in density and atomic number, so contrast depends on photoelectric absorption, which falls off quickly as energy rises. Molybdenum or rhodium targets and matching filters keep the spectrum in that useful low range."},
{id:"u6l5-4",t:"u6l5",q:"A weekly mammography phantom image scores 0.8 lower than the previous week's image. What does the program require?",a:["Adjust technique to recover the score and continue","Rescore the previous image","Stop and investigate, because a drop of more than 0.5 is a failure to be corrected","Wait for the next weekly image to confirm"],c:2,e:"The minimum to pass accreditation is four fibers, three speck groups, and three masses, and a score falling more than 0.5 from the last image is a stop-and-investigate finding. Look at calibration, target and filter selection, compression, and processing rather than at technique."},
{id:"u6l5-5",t:"u6l5",q:"Where does the automatic exposure detector sit in a mammography unit, and why does that placement matter?",a:["In front of the receptor, so it measures the beam before attenuation","On the collimator, facing the tube","Inside the compression paddle","Behind the receptor, so it measures what passed through and responds to beam quality as well as quantity"],c:3,e:"The mammographic exposure detector is placed behind the image receptor, so it reads the radiation that got all the way through and is sensitive to the quality of the beam as well as its quantity. That is why an exposure complaint here points at detector position, cell selection, or calibration."},

// u6l6
{id:"u6l6-1",t:"u6l6",q:"During a helical scan the couch advances 14 mm for each 360 deg gantry rotation with a beam width of 10 mm. What is the pitch?",a:["1.4:1","0.71:1","2.4:1","14:1"],c:0,e:"Pitch is couch travel per rotation divided by beam width: 14 / 10 = 1.4:1. A pitch above 1.0 stretches the helix so coverage is faster and dose lower, while below 1.0 the turns overlap and dose rises."},
{id:"u6l6-2",t:"u6l6",q:"A daily water phantom on a scanner now reads plus 9 instead of zero in the center. What does that tell you?",a:["The phantom has absorbed contrast and should be replaced","Calibration has drifted, because water is defined as exactly zero on the scale","The window level is set wrong on the console","The slice thickness is too thin for the phantom"],c:1,e:"The density scale is anchored with air near -1000, water at exactly 0, and dense bone up to about +3000, so a water reading away from zero is calibration evidence. Run the air and water calibrations on the manufacturer's schedule before anything clinical is questioned."},
{id:"u6l6-3",t:"u6l6",q:"Explaining a scanner gantry to a new technician, what is the function of the slip ring?",a:["It is the bearing that supports the couch","It holds the detector array in alignment with the tube","It carries power in and data out through brushes so the gantry can rotate continuously","It is the collimator that sets slice thickness"],c:2,e:"Brushes riding on rotating rings pass power and signals without a cable that would have to wind and unwind, which is what made continuous rotation and helical scanning possible. The brushes and the wear dust they make are service items."},
{id:"u6l6-4",t:"u6l6",q:"A dose report lists both a computed tomography dose index and a dose length product. How do the two differ?",a:["The index is for the tube and the product is for the detector","They are the same quantity in different units","The product is measured and the index is estimated","The index is dose in a slice spread over a defined 10 cm length, and the product multiplies it by the length scanned"],c:3,e:"The dose index accounts for radiation delivered beyond the chosen slice by spreading it over a defined 10 cm length, while the dose length product scales that index with how much of the patient was covered. Both are measured with pencil ionization chambers in standard phantoms."},
{id:"u6l6-5",t:"u6l6",q:"A department asks which scanner tasks are yours and which belong to the vendor under the service contract. Which split is typical?",a:["The technician handles table, alignment, covers, cooling, filters, and calibrations on schedule, while tube replacement and deep calibrations usually go to the vendor","The technician replaces the tube and the vendor cleans the filters","Everything inside the gantry cover is the technician's work","The vendor owns the console and the technician owns reconstruction software"],c:0,e:"Routine mechanical, thermal, and calibration work is the technician's, because it is what keeps the schedule running. High-cost, high-risk work such as tube replacement is usually shared with or owned by the vendor, so read the contract before opening the gantry."},

// u7l1
{id:"u7l1-1",t:"u7l1",q:"A sonographer says the deep abdominal image on a high-frequency linear probe is unusable, though superficial vessels look excellent. What is going on?",a:["The probe has element dropout","Higher frequency gives finer detail but attenuates faster, so it cannot reach that depth","The time-gain compensation circuit has failed","The probe needs recalibration against a phantom"],c:1,e:"Sound weakens with depth and weakens faster at higher frequency, so a probe is chosen as the highest frequency that still reaches the target. Deep abdominal work belongs on a lower-frequency curved probe, which is a probe-selection matter rather than a fault."},
{id:"u7l1-2",t:"u7l1",q:"An image shows a dark band that stays in the same part of the display while the probe is swept across the patient. What does that indicate?",a:["Poor contact from insufficient gel","A gas shadow from bowel","Element dropout or a broken conductor in that probe's channel","A failing monitor backlight"],c:2,e:"A hardware artifact is fixed in the image because it belongs to the same elements every time, while a shadow from gas or bone moves with the anatomy. Confirm on a phantom, flex the cable, change ports, then fit a known good probe."},
{id:"u7l1-3",t:"u7l1",q:"Why is coupling gel between probe and skin not optional for an ultrasound examination?",a:["It cools the probe face during long studies","It raises the frequency of the transmitted pulse","It prevents static damage to the elements","Air is such a large change in acoustic impedance that it reflects nearly all the sound"],c:3,e:"Echoes come from changes in acoustic impedance, and the change from probe or skin to air is so great that almost nothing passes. Gel excludes that air, which is also why bowel gas and bone leave shadows behind them."},
{id:"u7l1-4",t:"u7l1",q:"A cardiologist needs images of the heart through the ribs from the chest wall. Which probe design suits that geometry?",a:["A phased array with a small footprint steered electronically","A curved array","A linear array","An endocavity probe"],c:0,e:"A phased array has a small face that fits in an intercostal space and steers the beam electronically rather than by element position, which is why it is the cardiac probe. Linear probes are superficial and curved probes are for abdomen and obstetrics."},
{id:"u7l1-5",t:"u7l1",q:"A probe begins producing noise and intermittent dropout a week after being soaked for disinfection. What is the most likely cause?",a:["Normal wear of the piezoelectric elements","Fluid ingress past the immersion line, or a wet connector","The system's beamformer drifting out of calibration","An outdated software version on the cart"],c:1,e:"Soaking a probe beyond its marked immersion line, or wetting the connector, lets fluid reach the interconnect and the fine cable conductors, and the failure often appears days later. The corrective action is retraining and relabeling the chemical and the immersion line, not just a replacement probe."},

// u7l2
{id:"u7l2-1",t:"u7l2",q:"A cart produces weak images on every probe it has, and one of those probes works normally on another cart. Where is the fault?",a:["In each of the probes","In the disinfection procedure","In the cart: transmit, receive, or processing","In the archive network link"],c:2,e:"Swapping probe and port splits the system from the probe in minutes. A probe that is good elsewhere proves the cart, so the fault lies in transmit, receive and beamforming, or processing rather than in the hand-held hardware."},
{id:"u7l2-2",t:"u7l2",q:"A new technologist asks what makes deep structures as bright as shallow ones on an ultrasound image. Which control does that?",a:["Overall gain","The frequency preset","The focus setting","Time-gain compensation, which amplifies by depth to offset attenuation"],c:3,e:"Overall gain amplifies everything equally, so it brightens noise along with signal. Time-gain compensation increases amplification with depth to make up for the attenuation deeper echoes suffered, and a badly set curve looks like a machine fault."},
{id:"u7l2-3",t:"u7l2",q:"An endocavity probe is used with a disposable cover during the study. What reprocessing does it still require afterward?",a:["Full high-level disinfection, because the cover does not replace it","Wiping with an approved cleaner only","Sterilization in an autoclave","Nothing, provided the cover was intact"],c:0,e:"A probe contacting mucous membranes needs high-level disinfection between patients, and a cover reduces but does not eliminate contamination. Use only a chemical listed for that probe and never take it past the marked immersion line."},
{id:"u7l2-4",t:"u7l2",q:"Doppler traces on one system are noisy and sometimes absent while gray scale images look acceptable. What should you check first?",a:["The cart battery capacity under load","Contact and gel, the preset, then whether a known good probe behaves the same","The monitor calibration against a test pattern","The network link to the archive"],c:1,e:"Doppler reads the small frequency shift from moving blood, so it degrades first when contact, gel, preset, or a receive channel is marginal. Prove it with a known good probe before suspecting the beamformer."},
{id:"u7l2-5",t:"u7l2",q:"What should an annual ultrasound quality control visit actually produce as evidence?",a:["A signed checklist with no attachments","A copy of the service contract","Phantom images at fixed settings filed with the probe inspection and display check","The error log printout alone"],c:2,e:"Penetration depth, resolution, and uniformity only mean something against the baseline images on file, so the images are the record. File them with the probe inspection, the display test pattern result, and confirmation that every control and footswitch works."},

// u7l3
{id:"u7l3-1",t:"u7l3",q:"A gamma camera's energy window is set around 140 keV. Which tracer is the system tuned for?",a:["Fluorine-18","Barium sulfate","Iodine-131","Technetium-99m"],c:3,e:"Technetium-99m decays to technetium-99 emitting a gamma ray of about 140 keV, which is the window the camera accepts so that scattered and unrelated photons are rejected. A drifting window counts the wrong photons and the image gets noisy."},
{id:"u7l3-2",t:"u7l3",q:"Removing the collimator from a gamma camera head would have what effect on the image?",a:["No image, because nothing would restrict which direction accepted photons came from","Higher spatial resolution at lower counts","A wider energy window","Better uniformity across the detector"],c:0,e:"The lead collimator accepts only gamma rays travelling along its hole axis, and that geometric selection is what forms the image. It is also heavy and easily dented, so collimators, changers, and carts are a mechanical safety item."},
{id:"u7l3-3",t:"u7l3",q:"In positron imaging, what does the detector ring actually count?",a:["Single photons of about 140 keV","Pairs of photons of about 0.51 MeV arriving in coincidence from an annihilation","Beta particles emitted by the tracer","Characteristic x-rays from the collimator"],c:1,e:"A positron from an agent such as fluorine-18 annihilates with an electron, producing two photons of about 0.51 MeV in opposite directions. Accepting only coincident pairs locates the event electronically, which is why no collimator is needed."},
{id:"u7l3-4",t:"u7l3",q:"A daily flood image on a gamma camera shows a cool circular patch that was not there last week. What does that point to?",a:["An expired radiopharmaceutical","A cracked couch top","A photomultiplier tube or its tuning","A network fault to the archive"],c:2,e:"Uniformity across the detector depends on the photomultiplier array and its gains, so a localized cool or hot area in a flood image points there. A repeating pattern instead suggests a damaged collimator, and a general loss of resolution suggests the crystal or its light coupling."},
{id:"u7l3-5",t:"u7l3",q:"Which nuclear medicine task falls outside a radiology equipment technician's scope?",a:["Gantry motion and collimator changer maintenance","Computer and network link service","Table motion and covers","Calibrating the dose calibrator and handling the licensed radioactive material"],c:3,e:"The radioactive material is licensed to the facility, and the dose calibrator, surveys, wipe tests, and records belong to the physicist and the licensee. Detector heads, gantry, collimator handling hardware, table, cooling, and computers are the technician's work."},

// u7l4
{id:"u7l4-1",t:"u7l4",q:"You need to take a digital multimeter into a magnet room to check a table motor. What governs whether you may?",a:["The meter's label: MR Safe, MR Conditional with stated limits, or MR Unsafe","Whether the scanner is between patients","Whether you stay outside the 5-gauss line","The field strength written on the magnet"],c:0,e:"Every item taken into the room must be labeled, and conditional items are safe only within the field, gradient, and radiofrequency limits they were tested to. An unlabeled meter is treated as unsafe, because the field is always on and ferromagnetic objects become projectiles."},
{id:"u7l4-2",t:"u7l4",q:"A scanner reports a falling helium level and the cold head is warm to the touch. How urgent is that call?",a:["Routine: add it to the next scheduled visit","Same-day escalation to the vendor, because losing cryogen threatens superconduction","Non-urgent as long as imaging continues","It can wait until the next physicist survey"],c:1,e:"The compressor, cold head, and chiller keep the coil superconducting, and once the helium falls far enough the magnet can quench. Helium alarms and a stopped cold head are emergencies, and the fill and cold head service belong to the vendor."},
{id:"u7l4-3",t:"u7l4",q:"Images from a magnet show repeating interference lines that appeared after cabling work in the room. What is the FIRST thing to examine?",a:["Gradient amplifier calibration","The receive coil element count","The shielded room: the door seal, the filter panel, and any new penetration","The helium level"],c:2,e:"The copper enclosure and its filter panel keep outside radio energy out, so a door that does not seal or an unfiltered penetration lets interference into the image. Cabling work in or through the room is the obvious suspect when the artifact is new."},
{id:"u7l4-4",t:"u7l4",q:"A technologist reports warmth under a surface coil cable during scans. Why is that treated as a safety finding?",a:["Cryogen is leaking along the cable sheath","The cable is carrying the static magnetic field","The gradient coils transfer heat down the cable","Deposited radiofrequency energy heats tissue, and looped or damaged coil cables cause burns"],c:3,e:"Radiofrequency energy delivered during imaging heats tissue, and a damaged or looped coil cable concentrates it. Coil and cable inspection is a scheduled item for exactly this reason, and a suspect coil comes out of service."},
{id:"u7l4-5",t:"u7l4",q:"Which set of magnet room tasks is normally the technician's rather than the manufacturer's?",a:["Chiller and water loop service, filters, coil inspection, table motion, and room integrity","Replacing gradient coils","Ramping the magnet and shimming the field","Cryogen filling and cold head replacement"],c:0,e:"Cooling plant, filters, receive coils and cables, table motion, rated peripherals, room shielding, power conditioning, and the console side are the technician's. Anything that could ramp or quench the magnet, including cold head service and cryogen fills, stays with the manufacturer."},

// u8l1
{id:"u8l1-1",t:"u8l1",q:"A quality control visit measures 70 kVp with the console set to 80 kVp. Does the room pass the kVp accuracy test?",a:["Yes, because any reading within 15 kVp is acceptable","No: the tolerance is 10 percent, so 8 kVp at this setting, and the error is 10 kVp","Yes, because only the half-value layer has a legal limit","No, because measured kVp must match the setting exactly"],c:1,e:"Measured kVp must stay within plus or minus 10 percent of the indicated value, which at 80 kVp allows 8 kVp. A 10 kVp error fails, and it also explains flat, grainy images, so record the measurement with its tolerance and recalibrate."},
{id:"u8l1-2",t:"u8l1",q:"The high-voltage generator in a general room has just been repaired. Which quality control work is required before the room returns to clinical use?",a:["Nothing until the annual due date","A full mammography-style daily schedule","Repeat the output tests that the repair could have affected: kVp, timer, linearity, reproducibility","Only an electrical safety test"],c:2,e:"Evaluation follows any major equipment modification, so a console, generator, or tube repair resets the clock on the affected output tests. Waiting for the anniversary leaves a room running on unverified output."},
{id:"u8l1-3",t:"u8l1",q:"Which radiographic quality control tolerance pair is stated correctly?",a:["Both are held within 2 percent of the source-to-image distance","Linearity within 2 percent, reproducibility within 20 percent","Linearity within 20 percent, reproducibility within 10 percent","Linearity within 10 percent between adjacent mA stations, and reproducibility within plus or minus 5 percent"],c:3,e:"Output per mAs must agree within 10 percent between adjacent mA stations, and repeated identical exposures must agree within plus or minus 5 percent. The 2 percent figures belong to collimation, automatic collimation, and the distance indicator instead."},
{id:"u8l1-4",t:"u8l1",q:"A department asks you to explain the difference between its technologists' daily checks, your scheduled quality control, and the medical physicist's annual visit.",a:["Daily checks and your scheduled measurements are repetitive and comparative, while the physicist's survey is an independent evaluation producing findings to close","They are three names for the same inspection","The physicist performs the preventive maintenance and you perform the survey","Only the physicist's visit has to be documented"],c:0,e:"Your measurements prove continued performance against tolerances using the same method each time, and the technologists' checks catch day-to-day drift. The physicist's annual survey independently evaluates dose, shielding, and image quality against the accreditation standard, and its findings have closure dates."},
{id:"u8l1-5",t:"u8l1",q:"Which item belongs on an imaging preventive maintenance procedure but is most often left undone?",a:["Wiping the covers","Reading the error log and recording the entries before clearing them","Checking the room lights","Updating the asset tag"],c:1,e:"The log is the equipment's own account of intermittent faults, and clearing it without recording destroys evidence. Note firmware versions at the same time, and patch only on the vendor's validated schedule."},

// u8l2
{id:"u8l2-1",t:"u8l2",q:"A radiographer insists the generator is fine because the console shows 80 kVp. How do you answer?",a:["Console agreement within 20 percent is acceptable","The pre-reading voltmeter is the reference standard","The console display is a selection, not a measurement, so the tube potential has to be verified with a meter","Only the half-value layer can be measured, not kVp"],c:2,e:"The console shows the autotransformer tap that was chosen, before any exposure is made. A non-invasive meter in the beam reports the kVp the tube actually produced, along with exposure time, dose, dose rate, and half-value layer."},
{id:"u8l2-2",t:"u8l2",q:"You need to measure the half-value layer on a room operating at 80 kVp. What is the method?",a:["Read the filtration thickness from the tube housing label","Measure the exposure at two distances and apply the inverse square law","Double the exposure and measure the difference","Measure output, then add calibrated aluminum sheets until the reading falls to half of the original, and compare with the minimum for that kVp and waveform"],c:3,e:"Inherent and added filtration cannot be measured separately on an assembled tube, so beam quality stands in for both. The thickness of aluminum that halves the intensity is compared against a table indexed by kVp and waveform, because the minimum rises with both."},
{id:"u8l2-3",t:"u8l2",q:"Which instrument is mounted under the collimator to track dose during an interventional case?",a:["A dose area product meter, a flat transmission chamber that reads dose multiplied by field area","A pencil ionization chamber","A survey meter","A thermoluminescent badge"],c:0,e:"The dose area product meter sits in the beam path at the collimator and reads dose times the irradiated area, so collimating tighter lowers the number. A pencil chamber belongs to computed tomography dose measurements and a survey meter to room scatter."},
{id:"u8l2-4",t:"u8l2",q:"A dosimeter reports a fluoroscopic tabletop rate of 95 mGy/min. How does that compare with the normal-operation limit?",a:["It exceeds the limit and the room must be removed from service","It is just under the limit, because 10 R/min is about 100 mGy/min","It cannot be compared, because the units are different quantities","It is about half the limit"],c:1,e:"Normal fluoroscopic operation is limited to 10 R/min at the tabletop, which is about 100 mGy/min, so 95 mGy/min is just inside. Convert without hesitating, because meters report in gray while many regulations are written in roentgen."},
{id:"u8l2-5",t:"u8l2",q:"An inspector finds that the dosimeter used for last year's output measurements had an expired calibration certificate. What is the consequence?",a:["The readings stand because the meter was working","Only future measurements are affected","The records made with that meter cannot be relied on, so the measurements must be repeated with a calibrated instrument","The facility may recalculate the readings using a correction factor"],c:2,e:"A measurement is only as good as the instrument, so an out-of-calibration meter retroactively invalidates the records it produced. Keep current certificates with the test equipment inventory, because a surveyor asks for them alongside the readings."},

// u8l3
{id:"u8l3-1",t:"u8l3",q:"Testing a mobile x-ray unit with a safety analyzer, you measure 0.8 ohm from chassis to the plug ground pin. What is the verdict?",a:["It passes, because the limit is 1 ohm","Ground resistance does not apply to imaging equipment","It passes if chassis leakage is acceptable","It fails: ground resistance for patient-care equipment must not exceed 0.5 ohm"],c:3,e:"NFPA 99 holds ground resistance from chassis to the plug ground pin at 0.5 ohm maximum for patient-care equipment, because that path has to carry fault and leakage current. Check the cord, plug, and strain relief, since a damaged ground conductor is the usual cause."},
{id:"u8l3-2",t:"u8l3",q:"Why does the analyzer sequence include a measurement with the ground deliberately opened?",a:["Because leakage normally returns through the ground wire, so the open-ground condition shows what a person would be exposed to if that path were lost","To test the breaker","To measure insulation resistance","Because the limit is higher with the ground open"],c:0,e:"Leakage current rides the equipment grounding conductor, and an open ground turns an invisible leak into a touch hazard. The sequence therefore measures chassis leakage in normal polarity, reversed polarity, and with the ground open."},
{id:"u8l3-3",t:"u8l3",q:"An interventional table and its ceiling-mounted gantry are permanently wired, so there is no plug to lift. What replaces the leakage sequence?",a:["Nothing: hard-wired equipment is exempt from electrical safety work","Ground and bonding continuity from chassis to the ground reference, insulation and conductor condition, the disconnect and overcurrent device, and the manufacturer's installation checks","A ground resistance measurement taken at the nearest receptacle","An insulation test at 500 V across the mains input"],c:1,e:"With no plug there is no polarity to reverse and no plug pin to measure against, so the substitute is proving the grounding and bonding path and the installation itself. Record the substitution and its readings explicitly, because a blank where leakage belongs reads as an omission."},
{id:"u8l3-4",t:"u8l3",q:"Which applied part leakage limits apply to a catheter-based connection in a catheterization laboratory?",a:["Type B, 500 microamps normal and 1 mA single fault","Type BF, 100 microamps normal and 500 microamps single fault","Type CF, 10 microamps normal and 50 microamps single fault","Earth leakage, 5 mA normal and 10 mA single fault"],c:2,e:"A direct connection to the heart is a type CF applied part, with the tightest limits in IEC 60601-1 at 10 and 50 microamps. The reason is microshock: as little as 10 microamps delivered directly to the heart can cause fibrillation."},
{id:"u8l3-5",t:"u8l3",q:"You need to check an interlock contact and a low-voltage rail inside a generator cabinet. What is the correct practice?",a:["Measure continuity with the equipment energized so the circuit is realistic","Rely on the console fault codes instead of measuring","Use the same meter on the high-voltage section for comparison","Measure resistance and continuity only with the equipment off and capacitors discharged and verified at zero, and voltage only when the circuit must be live and the room is clear"],c:3,e:"Stored energy in a generator cabinet is lethal, so resistance work is done dead and verified at zero before anything is touched. Tube potential is measured non-invasively from the beam side, and a high-voltage divider is used only where the manufacturer supplies one."}
);
